<header><p class="st-kicker">electorate · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Electorate of Salzburg</h1><p class="st-meta">1803 CE – 1805 CE · capital Salzburg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Short-lived electorate created from the secularised archbishopric of Salzburg for a Habsburg prince who had lost Tuscany.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Archbishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hieronymus-von-colloredo">Hieronymus von Colloredo</a> fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a> before French troops in 1800. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichsdeputationshauptschluss">Imperial Recess of 1803</a> the prince-archbishopric was secularised and given, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berchtesgaden">Berchtesgaden</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passau">Passau</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eichstatt">Eichstätt</a>, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-iii-grand-duke-of-tuscany">Ferdinand III</a>, the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duke-of-tuscany">Grand Duke of Tuscany</a>, who received an electoral title.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Ferdinand's brief rule brought a reorganised administration and a secular government in place of the archbishops, though the population remained Catholic. The university was continued, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg's</a> economy still relied on salt mining at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hallein">Hallein</a> and trade. The electorate had little time to develop before war returned.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria's</a> defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-austerlitz">Austerlitz</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-pressburg-1805">Peace of Pressburg</a> in December 1805 gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a> to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a>, and Ferdinand was compensated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a>. Salzburg later passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> from 1810 to 1816 before returning permanently to Austria.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/electorate-of-salzburg.jpg" alt="Electorate of Salzburg" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Salzburg,_Vienna,_Vorarlberg.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1803 CE – 1805 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>electorate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Salzburg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ferdinand III</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>, <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kurfürstentum Salzburg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1804&amp;focus=electorate-of-salzburg" aria-label="Electorate of Salzburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4984 503 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Electorate of Salzburg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5304 759L5304 759M5304 759L5229 694M5304 759L5381 747M5304 759L5228 700M5304 759L5304 764"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 759h0M5229 694h0M5381 747h0M5228 700h0M5304 764h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 759h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 759h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Salzburg. <a href="/atlas?year=1804&amp;focus=electorate-of-salzburg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
